    "spec_subtitle": "Complete technical reference for the Industrial Asset Event Standard.",
    "sec_purpose": "1. Purpose",
    "purpose_p1": "IAES defines how industrial asset signals, diagnoses, and maintenance intents are represented and transferred across heterogeneous operational systems.",
    "purpose_p2": "IAES is not a product. It is a data contract. Any system — sensor platform, AI engine, CMMS, historian, or dashboard — can produce or consume IAES events without knowing the implementation details of the other side.",
    "sec_principles": "2. Principles",
    "principle_1_title": "Vendor neutrality",
    "principle_1_desc": "No dependency on SAP, PI System, Odoo, or any specific vendor.",
    "principle_2_title": "Legacy compatibility",
    "principle_2_desc": "Events map cleanly to CMMS, historians, SCADA, and IoT platforms.",
    "principle_3_title": "Event-oriented",
    "principle_3_desc": "Each object represents something that happened (measurement, diagnosis, intent).",
    "principle_4_title": "Complete traceability",
    "principle_4_desc": "Every event traces back to its origin via event_id, correlation_id, source_event_id.",
    "principle_5_title": "Extensibility",
    "principle_5_desc": "The data payload and metadata field allow new fields without breaking consumers.",
    "sec_terminology": "3. Terminology",
    "sec_envelope": "4. Common Envelope",
    "envelope_intro": "Every IAES event is wrapped in a common envelope. The envelope carries identity, traceability, and routing information. The event-specific payload lives inside the data field.",
    "envelope_fields_title": "Field Definitions",
    "asset_identity_title": "Asset Identity",
    "asset_identity_desc": "The asset block identifies the target asset. Only asset_id is required; the remaining fields provide human-readable context and hierarchical location.",
    "sec_events": "5. Event Types",
    "events_intro": "IAES v1.3 defines 7 event types organized into 3 domains: asset signals, maintenance actions, and sensor lifecycle.",
    "measurement_desc": "A single measurement reading from an asset. This is the most fundamental event type — it captures raw sensor data from vibration, temperature, current, pressure, or any other measurable quantity.",
    "health_desc": "A health assessment for an asset. Produced by AI engines, rule-based systems, or human experts. Links to the measurement(s) that triggered it via source_event_id and correlation_id.",
    "wo_intent_desc": "A recommendation to create a maintenance work order. This is an intent, not a work order itself. The receiving CMMS decides whether to accept, modify, or reject it. This decoupling is fundamental to IAES — the AI or rule engine recommends, the CMMS decides.",
    "completion_desc": "Reports the completion (or cancellation/deferral) of a maintenance activity. Links back to the original work order via correlation_id and provides outcome data for reliability analytics.",
    "hierarchy_desc": "Declares or updates the hierarchical position of an asset within an organization. Used to synchronize asset trees across systems (CMMS, historians, digital twins).",
    "sensor_desc": "Declares or updates a sensor's lifecycle state. Tracks sensors from discovery through calibration to decommissioning, enabling fleet management and audit trails for measurement provenance.",
    "spare_part_desc": "Records the consumption of a spare part during a maintenance activity. Links to the work order via work_order_id and enables inventory tracking, cost analytics, and procurement automation.",
    "sec_severity": "6. Severity Levels",
    "severity_intro": "IAES defines 5 severity levels used in asset.health events. These represent the condition of the asset, not the urgency of the maintenance response.",
    "sec_idempotency": "7. Idempotency",
    "idempotency_intro": "IAES provides three mechanisms for deduplication and idempotent processing:",
    "sec_producer": "8. Producer Guidelines",
    "producer_required": "Required",
    "producer_recommended": "Recommended",
    "sec_consumer": "9. Consumer Guidelines",
    "consumer_required": "Required",
    "consumer_recommended": "Recommended",
    "sec_flow": "10. Typical Flow",
    "flow_intro": "A typical IAES event chain follows this pattern. All events in the chain share the same correlation_id, and each event references its direct predecessor via source_event_id.",
    "sec_compat": "11. System Compatibility",
    "compat_intro": "IAES events are designed to map naturally to common industrial systems. The table below shows how IAES concepts map to each platform.",
    "sec_appendix_a": "Appendix A: Failure Mode Taxonomy",
    "appendix_a_intro": "Standardized failure mode values for the failure_mode field in asset.health and maintenance.completion events. Organized by equipment domain.",
    "sec_appendix_b": "Appendix B: ISO 14224 Codes",
    "appendix_b_intro": "The iso_14224 object provides standardized failure classification per ISO 14224:2016. This object can appear in asset.health and maintenance.completion events.",
    "sec_appendix_c": "Appendix C: ISO 13374 Mapping",
    "appendix_c_intro": "ISO 13374 defines a 6-block processing model for condition monitoring. IAES maps naturally to this standard. The iso_13374_status field in asset.health events provides explicit alignment.",
