    "cmp.confusion_title": "The Confusion",
    "cmp.confusion_subtitle": "Why engineers keep comparing four technologies that solve completely different problems",
    "cmp.confusion_p1": "Engineers frequently compare OPC UA, Sparkplug B, the Asset Administration Shell (AAS), and <a href=\"/what-is-iaes.html\">IAES</a> as if they are competing standards. They are not. They operate at different layers of the industrial data stack, solving different problems for different audiences.",
    "cmp.confusion_p2": "Comparing IAES to Sparkplug B is like comparing SQL to TCP/IP. One defines <strong>what data means</strong>, the other defines <strong>how data moves</strong>. Comparing IAES to AAS is like comparing a medical record to an anatomy textbook. One captures <strong>what happened</strong> over time, the other describes <strong>what the thing is</strong>.",
    "cmp.confusion_p3": "The confusion is understandable. All four touch industrial IoT. All four deal with assets and data. But the overlap is superficial. In practice, they are complementary. Many production architectures use two or three of them simultaneously, each at its own layer.",
    "cmp.confusion_p4": "This page explains what each standard does, where it sits in the stack, and how to choose. If you already know you need event semantics across systems, go directly to the <a href=\"/spec/\">IAES specification</a>. If you are still figuring out which layer your problem lives in, read on.",
    "cmp.matrix_title": "Side-by-Side Comparison",
    "cmp.matrix_subtitle": "Nine dimensions, four standards. The differences become obvious once you see them together.",
