    "iso.iso17359_title": "ISO 17359 \u2014 Condition Monitoring & Diagnostics",
    "iso.iso17359_subtitle": "Three fields added in IAES v1.2 for ISO 17359 measurement compliance.",
    "iso.iso17359_intro": "ISO 17359 defines how vibration, temperature, and other condition monitoring measurements should be recorded for diagnostic purposes. A vibration reading of \u201c4.5 mm/s\u201d is ambiguous without context: is it RMS or peak? What was the sampling rate? How long was the measurement window? These details matter for accurate diagnosis and trending.",
    "iso.iso17359_fields": "IAES v1.2 added three optional fields to <code>asset.measurement</code> that resolve these ambiguities:",
    "iso.field_units_title": "<code>units_qualifier</code>",
    "iso.field_units_desc": "Resolves the most common ambiguity in vibration measurements. When you see \u201c4.5 mm/s\u201d, is that RMS or peak? The qualifier makes it explicit. Accepted values: <code>rms</code>, <code>peak</code>, <code>peak_to_peak</code>, <code>average</code>, <code>true_rms</code>. This field applies to any measurement type, not just vibration \u2014 temperature, current, pressure all benefit from explicit qualification.",
    "iso.field_sampling_title": "<code>sampling_rate_hz</code>",
    "iso.field_sampling_desc": "Sample rate in Hz. Critical for vibration analysis due to the Nyquist theorem: to detect a 10 kHz bearing defect frequency, you need at least 20 kHz sampling. Recording the sampling rate means downstream systems can validate whether the measurement is trustworthy for a given analysis. Example: <code>25600</code> Hz for high-frequency bearing detection.",
    "iso.field_duration_title": "<code>acquisition_duration_s</code>",
    "iso.field_duration_desc": "Duration of the measurement window in seconds. A 0.1-second capture misses low-frequency phenomena. A 10-second capture provides better frequency resolution but larger data. Recording this value enables apples-to-apples trending: you can only compare measurements taken with the same acquisition parameters. Example: <code>2.0</code> seconds for standard vibration capture.",
    "iso.iso17359_example": "Here is an <code>asset.measurement</code> event with all three ISO 17359 fields populated:",
    "iso.iso17359_zones_intro": "ISO 17359 also defines a <strong>4-zone alarm system</strong> (Zones A through D) for classifying machine vibration severity. IAES maps these zones through the <code>severity</code> field on <code>asset.health</code> events:",
    "iso.col_zone": "ISO 17359 Zone",
    "iso.col_zone_meaning": "Meaning",
    "iso.col_iaes_severity": "IAES <code>severity</code>",
    "iso.zone_a": "Zone A \u2014 Good",
    "iso.zone_a_meaning": "Newly commissioned or excellent condition",
    "iso.zone_b": "Zone B \u2014 Acceptable",
    "iso.zone_b_meaning": "Long-term operation acceptable",
    "iso.zone_c": "Zone C \u2014 Alert",
    "iso.zone_c_meaning": "Not suitable for sustained operation",
    "iso.zone_d": "Zone D \u2014 Danger",
    "iso.zone_d_meaning": "Risk of damage, shutdown recommended",
    "iso.iso14224_title": "ISO 14224 \u2014 Failure Classification",
    "iso.iso14224_subtitle": "Standardized failure codes for reliability data collection, embedded directly in IAES events.",
    "iso.iso14224_intro": "ISO 14224 defines standardized codes for recording <em>what</em> failed, <em>why</em> it failed, and <em>how</em> the failure was detected. This data is essential for reliability engineering: calculating MTBF, identifying systemic failure patterns, and justifying capital investment in predictive maintenance. Without standardized codes, failure data across plants and systems cannot be compared or aggregated.",
    "iso.iso14224_iaes": "IAES v1.2 added an optional <code>iso_14224</code> object to both <code>asset.health</code> and <code>maintenance.completion</code> events. This creates a powerful feedback loop: the AI system predicts a failure mode, and the technician confirms or corrects it after repair.",
    "iso.iso14224_loop": "<strong>The feedback loop works like this:</strong> An AI engine analyzes vibration data and emits an <code>asset.health</code> event with <code>iso_14224.mechanism_code: \"BRG\"</code> (bearing failure predicted). A work order is created. The technician replaces the bearing and closes the work order. The <a href=\"/iaes-vs-sparkplug-vs-aas/\">bridge</a> emits a <code>maintenance.completion</code> event \u2014 if the technician confirmed bearing wear, the same <code>BRG</code> code appears. If the actual root cause was misalignment, the technician corrects it to <code>ALN</code>. Both events share a <code>correlation_id</code>, so the ML model can learn from the correction.",
    "iso.iso14224_taxonomy_intro": "<strong>Failure Mode Taxonomy (IAES Appendix A):</strong> IAES defines standard <code>failure_mode</code> values based on ISO 14224, organized by equipment category:",
    "iso.taxonomy_rotating": "Rotating Equipment",
    "iso.taxonomy_electrical": "Electrical",
    "iso.taxonomy_fluid_thermal": "Fluid / Thermal",
    "iso.cta_title": "Start Building with IAES",
